\title{Naive Psychometric DIF Transfer to LLM Populations Fails:\\Architecture Heterogeneity and Construct Misspecification\\as Structural Failure Modes}

\author{Anonymous}

\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% ABSTRACT
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
Differential Item Functioning (DIF), a classical psychometric tool for detecting item-level behavioral anomalies, has been proposed for detecting contamination-driven instability in LLM benchmarks. We apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and initially observe that 32.1\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. However, architecture-stratified analysis reveals this headline result is predominantly an artifact of population heterogeneity: restricting to decoder-only models reduces temporal DIF to 9.7\%, and Item-Focused Trees identify architecture type---not release date---as the primary splitting variable for 3,847 of 4,259 flagged items. Furthermore, IRT model fit remains poor even within architecturally homogeneous subpopulations (RMSEA$=$0.063, CFI$=$0.84), and bifactor models fail to converge (23 Heywood cases across 57 MMLU subjects), demonstrating that the psychometric framework itself is misspecified for LLM populations. Bayesian accumulation of evidence from the misspecified model converges confidently to wrong answers (Type-S error rate$=$0.31). We document three structural failure modes: (1)~architecture heterogeneity inflates DIF by $\sim$70\%, (2)~multidimensional construct structure violates unidimensional IRT assumptions, and (3)~LLM response patterns violate Item Characteristic Curve smoothness. As positive contributions, we validate a semi-synthetic framework for evaluating item-level contamination detectors ($\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 on MMLU, +0.69 on GSM8K with external matching), and argue that the field's standard validation against web-overlap labels is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection (enrichment$\approx$1.0). Code and data are available upon publication.
\end{abstract}

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 1. INTRODUCTION
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: when ``Model~B outperforms Model~A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under equivalent conditions. As the number of publicly available models grows into the thousands, this assumption becomes increasingly difficult to verify. Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis---a psychometric technique designed to detect items that behave differently across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability---offers a principled framework for testing this assumption at the item level.

We report the results of applying Mantel-Haenszel (MH) DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix, comprising 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items. This constitutes the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our initial finding---that 32.1\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF---appears to support contamination-driven instability as a major threat to benchmark validity. However, systematic investigation reveals this result is primarily an artifact of architecture heterogeneity in the model population.

This paper makes three contributions, each grounded in negative results that we believe are informative for the community:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Empirical characterization of structural failure modes.} We document why naive psychometric DIF transfer to LLM populations fails: architecture heterogeneity inflates DIF by approximately 70\%, IRT models are misspecified even within homogeneous subpopulations, and Bayesian sophistication cannot rescue a misspecified likelihood.
    \item \textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} We argue that the field's standard validation of contamination detectors against web-overlap labels is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection methods. DIF enrichment$\approx$1.0 against such labels is expected, not evidence of failure.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We develop and validate a reusable infrastructure for evaluating any item-level contamination detector via controlled differential contamination injection ($\Delta$TPR methodology), demonstrating its portability across MMLU and GSM8K.
\end{enumerate}

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 2. BACKGROUND
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Background}

\subsection{Differential Item Functioning}

An item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly. The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure tests for uniform DIF by stratifying examinees into ability-matched groups (using total score) and computing an odds ratio across strata. ETS classifies items into three categories based on both statistical significance and effect size $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}|$: A~(negligible, $<$1.0), B~(moderate, 1.0--1.5), and C~(large, $\geq$1.5). We focus on Category~C items as indicators of substantial differential functioning.

\subsection{IRT and Model Fit}

Item Response Theory (IRT) models the probability of a correct response as a function of a latent ability parameter $\theta$ via an Item Characteristic Curve (ICC). Standard fit indices include RMSEA (Root Mean Square Error of Approximation; acceptable $<$0.05, mediocre 0.05--0.08, poor $>$0.08), CFI (Comparative Fit Index; acceptable $>$0.95, mediocre 0.90--0.95), and SRMR (acceptable $<$0.08). Bifactor models posit a general factor plus group-specific factors.

\subsection{LLM Benchmark Contamination Detection}

Existing methods include web-overlap analysis, n-gram matching, perturbation-based approaches (ConStat), and generation variance (DVD). These methods typically validate against web-overlap or n-gram labels as ground truth.

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 3. METHOD
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Method}

\subsection{Data}

We use the METABENCH response matrix: 5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items (binary correct/incorrect). Models span release dates from 2023 to mid-2024, architectures including decoder-only, encoder-decoder, and mixture-of-experts (MoE), and training methods including SFT, alignment RL, and reasoning RL.

\subsection{Cohort Definitions}

\textbf{Temporal cohorts.} Reference: models released before a cutoff date (2023); Focal: models released after (2024-H1). \textbf{Architecture subgroups.} Decoder-only ($n{=}3{,}412$), encoder-decoder, and MoE subsets. \textbf{Within-family pairs.} Llama-2$\to$3, Mistral$\to$Mixtral.

\subsection{MH-DIF Procedure}

For each item, we compute the MH odds ratio across total-score strata, convert to the $\Delta_{\text{MH}}$ scale, and classify using ETS thresholds. C\% denotes the percentage of items classified as ETS Category~C.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Injection}

We inject differential contamination by flipping a fraction $p$ of focal group responses on designated items from incorrect to correct. Detection power is measured as $\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$. Two matching strategies are compared: standard (within-subject total score) and external (total score on other subjects or external benchmark).

\subsection{Architecture Stratification and Item-Focused Trees}

To disentangle architecture effects from temporal effects, we (a)~restrict DIF analysis to architecturally homogeneous subpopulations, and (b)~apply Item-Focused Trees (IFT), which use recursive partitioning on model covariates to identify the primary driver of item response differences.

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 4. EXPERIMENTS
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experiments}

%% 4.1 Naive Full-Population DIF
\subsection{Full-Population DIF on MMLU}
\label{sec:exp-naive}

We first apply MH-DIF to the full, architecture-mixed population as a baseline.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF detection by cohort definition and item filtering on the full METABENCH-MMLU population. Temporal C\% remains $\sim$30--33\% regardless of negative-discrimination filtering, indicating the signal is not item-quality-driven.}
\label{tab:naive-dif}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{C\%} & \textbf{Notes} \\
\midrule
Temporal cohort (full) & 32.1 & Primary estimate \\
Ability-matched cohort & 3.1 & Negligible ability-driven DIF \\
Random split (sanity) & 0.21 & Confirms non-trivial signal \\
\midrule
Within-family: Llama-2$\to$3 & 58.7 & Major architecture revision \\
Within-family: Mistral$\to$Mixtral & 41.2 & MoE transition \\
\midrule
Domain $\rho$ (DIF $\sim$ accuracy gain) & \multicolumn{2}{c}{$\rho{=}0.29,\ p{=}0.028$} \\
DIF--ability Jaccard & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.016 (near-orthogonal)} \\
Enrichment vs.\ Li et al.\ labels & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.97 ($\approx$1.0)} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At face value, 32.1\% temporal C\% suggests substantial item-level instability. The near-zero sanity check (0.21\%) confirms the signal is non-trivial. However, several warning signs emerge: (a)~within-family DIF tracks architecture changes (Llama-2$\to$3: 58.7\%, reflecting GQA and RoPE scaling changes) rather than training data shifts; (b)~enrichment against web-overlap labels is $\approx$1.0, indicating DIF flags are orthogonal to known contamination proxies; (c)~domain-level correlation is weak ($\rho{=}0.29$), showing no clustering in ``known contaminated'' subjects.

%% 4.2 Architecture Stratification
\subsection{Architecture Stratification Reveals the Confound}
\label{sec:exp-arch}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF statistics by architecture subgroup. Restricting to homogeneous populations dramatically reduces C\%, indicating architecture heterogeneity is the primary driver.}
\label{tab:arch-strat}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subgroup} & \textbf{$n$} & \textbf{C\%} & \textbf{RMSEA} & \textbf{CFI} & \textbf{SRMR} \\
\midrule
Full population & 5,227 & 32.1 & 0.089 & 0.74 & --- \\
Decoder-only & 3,412 & 9.7 & 0.063 & 0.84 & 0.071 \\
Encoder-decoder & --- & 41.3 & --- & --- & --- \\
MoE subset & --- & 35.8 & --- & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Restricting to decoder-only models reduces C\% from 32.1\% to 9.7\%---a 21.6 percentage point reduction, demonstrating that $\sim$69\% of the original DIF signal was driven by architecture heterogeneity. The decoder-only sanity check remains near zero (0.15\%). Partial correlation controlling domain accuracy drops to $r{=}0.02$ ($p{=}0.67$), indicating all residual DIF is explained by capability improvement across subjects, not item-level contamination.

Encoder-decoder (41.3\%) and MoE (35.8\%) subsets retain high C\% because they are small, architecturally heterogeneous populations (MoE models mix dense and sparse architectures).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IRT fit indices: full population vs.\ decoder-only. Decoder-only improves but remains in the ``poor fit'' range (RMSEA$>$0.05, CFI$<$0.90).}
\label{tab:irt-fit}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Population} & \textbf{RMSEA} & \textbf{CFI} & \textbf{TLI} & \textbf{SRMR} \\
\midrule
Full & 0.089 & 0.74 & --- & --- \\
Decoder-only & 0.063 & 0.84 & 0.82 & 0.071 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Even within the decoder-only subgroup, IRT fit remains poor. RMSEA$=$0.063 exceeds the 0.05 threshold for acceptable fit; CFI$=$0.84 falls below the 0.90 threshold. This indicates that unidimensional IRT is misspecified for LLM populations regardless of architecture mixing.

\textbf{Non-uniform DIF.} SIBTEST analysis within the decoder-only subgroup finds 14.2\% non-uniform DIF, with only 28\% overlap against MH flags. MH misses the majority of non-uniform effects, further undermining it as the primary detection method.

%% 4.3 Item-Focused Trees
\subsection{Item-Focused Trees: Architecture Dominates Time}
\label{sec:exp-ift}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Variable importance from Item-Focused Trees. Architecture type is the primary split for the vast majority of items.}
\label{tab:ift}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Variable} & \textbf{Importance Rank} & \textbf{Items as Primary Split} & \textbf{Mean Gini} \\
\midrule
Architecture type & 1 & 3,847 & 0.43 \\
Parameter count & 2 & --- & 0.28 \\
Release date & 3 & 412 & 0.17 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Item-Focused Trees provide model-free, data-driven decomposition of what drives item response differences. Architecture type is the primary split for 3,847 items versus only 412 for release date. When restricted to temporal splits only, the C\%-equivalent is 8.7\%---consistent with the 9.7\% from architecture-stratified DIF. The 412 items where time is the primary split cluster in subjects with genuine temporal curriculum effects. Agreement with categorical DIF is low (0.34), confirming that categorical temporal DIF conflates architecture with time.

%% 4.4 IRT Misspecification
\subsection{Bifactor IRT: Fundamental Misspecification}
\label{sec:exp-bifactor}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Model fit across IRT specifications on the full METABENCH-MMLU data. All specifications show poor fit (RMSEA$>$0.05, CFI$<$0.90). The full 57-factor bifactor model fails to converge.}
\label{tab:bifactor}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Specification} & \textbf{Converged} & \textbf{RMSEA} & \textbf{CFI} & \textbf{Heywood} \\
\midrule
Unidimensional (fallback) & Yes & 0.061 & 0.86 & 0 \\
5-factor reduced & Yes & 0.072 & 0.83 & --- \\
57-factor bifactor & No (847 iter) & --- & --- & 23 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The 57-factor bifactor model (general $+$ 57 subject-specific factors) fails to converge after 847 iterations, producing 23 Heywood cases (negative variance estimates) and 8 subjects with inadmissible parameters. Even reduced specifications show poor fit. Without a fitting bifactor model, anchor items cannot be identified, making IRT equating infeasible. This result demonstrates that standard IRT is fundamentally misspecified for LLM populations due to: (a)~within-architecture correlated residuals violating local independence, (b)~57 subjects exceeding feasible factor structures, and (c)~near-deterministic LLM response patterns violating smooth ICC assumptions.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Heywood cases by subject group. 23 of 57 subjects produce inadmissible parameter estimates in the bifactor specification.}
\label{tab:heywood}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Total Heywood cases & 23 / 57 subjects \\
Subjects with negative variance & 8 \\
Iterations before failure & 847 \\
Anchor items identified & 0 \\
Equating feasible & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% 4.5 Bayesian Accumulation
\subsection{Bayesian Accumulation of Misspecified Evidence}
\label{sec:exp-bayesian}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Power and FPR by number of accumulated administrations. Power increases monotonically but FPR remains high ($\sim$0.28), and Type-S error rate is 0.31---Bayesian updating converges confidently to wrong answers.}
\label{tab:bayesian}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Administrations} & \textbf{Power} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{Type-S Rate} & \textbf{BF Range} \\
\midrule
1 & 0.38 & --- & --- & --- \\
2 & 0.51 & --- & --- & --- \\
3 & 0.62 & --- & --- & --- \\
4 & 0.68 & 0.28 & 0.31 & [2.1, 8.4] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

If MH-DIF detects architecture effects rather than contamination, Bayesian accumulation of MH evidence across time points produces increasingly confident architecture-DIF detection. Power ``improves'' from 0.38 to 0.68, but this is power to detect the wrong signal. The FPR (0.28 after 4 administrations) reflects the persistent architecture confound. The Type-S error rate of 0.31 means that even when an item is correctly flagged as ``changed,'' the direction is often wrong because architecture effects are not monotonically temporal. Posterior concentration on spurious items reaches 0.73.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Type-S error analysis. 31\% of flagged items show incorrect direction of DIF---Bayesian confidence without accuracy.}
\label{tab:types}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Posterior concentration on spurious items & 0.73 \\
Type-S error rate & 0.31 \\
Prior sensitivity (BF range) & [2.1, 8.4] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This demonstrates a subtle failure mode: Bayesian methods improve precision of estimation, but when the model is misspecified, they converge confidently to wrong answers. More sophisticated statistical methods cannot fix a fundamental model misspecification.

%% 4.6 Alternative Detectors
\subsection{Comparison with Alternative Contamination Detectors}
\label{sec:exp-alternatives}

We compare DIF against two alternative item-level contamination detectors and their ensemble.

\subsubsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{ConStat detection rates by effect size. 217 reformulated items; detection concentrated in large effects.}
\label{tab:constat}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Non-RL} & \textbf{RL} & \textbf{Overall} \\
\midrule
AUROC & 0.68 & 0.44 & --- \\
FDR-controlled detections & \multicolumn{3}{c}{34 / 217 (15.7\%)} \\
Human quality rating & \multicolumn{3}{c}{4.1 / 5} \\
Effect size (median, IQR) & \multicolumn{3}{c}{0.42 [0.18, 0.71]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

ConStat provides a legitimate contamination signal (AUROC$=$0.68 on non-RL models) via a mechanistically grounded approach: memorized answers are brittle to reformulation. However, RL-trained models degrade ConStat's performance (AUROC$=$0.44), consistent with theoretical predictions. The DIF--ConStat overlap is near zero (Jaccard$=$0.09, $\chi^2{=}1.3$, $p{=}0.25$), consistent with DIF flags being architecture noise rather than contamination signal.

\subsubsection{DVD Generation Variance}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DVD detection metrics by contamination type (12 models $\times$ 500 items). Only verbatim contamination produces separation.}
\label{tab:dvd}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{AUROC} & \textbf{Detection Rate} \\
\midrule
Verbatim & 0.61 & 8.3\% \\
Variant & 0.47 & --- \\
\midrule
DIF--DVD Jaccard & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.06} \\
Compute (per model) & \multicolumn{2}{c}{6.2 GPU-hours} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DVD detects verbatim memorization at moderate AUROC (0.61) but fails on variant contamination (0.47). Generation variance for clean items ($\mu{=}0.34$) versus contaminated ($\mu{=}0.21$) shows only mild separation. DIF--DVD overlap (Jaccard$=$0.06) is near zero, again consistent with DIF flags being orthogonal to actual contamination.

\subsubsection{Ensemble Fusion}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Ensemble performance by configuration. No configuration exceeds the best single method (ConStat alone at 0.68). DIF weight is consistently suppressed to $\sim$0.03 by cross-validation.}
\label{tab:ensemble}
\begin{tabular}{lccl}
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{AUROC} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{Notes} \\
\midrule
2-of-3 majority & 0.52 & 0.04 & DIF is random voter \\
Weighted (3 methods) & 0.63 & 0.11 & DIF weight$=$0.03 \\
ConStat + DVD only & 0.66 & --- & Excludes DIF \\
Uniform weights & 0.55 & --- & DIF degrades \\
ConStat alone & 0.68 & --- & Best single method \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Cross-validated weights (5-fold):}} \\
DIF & \multicolumn{3}{l}{0.03 (range: 0.00--0.08)} \\
ConStat & \multicolumn{3}{l}{0.62} \\
DVD & \multicolumn{3}{l}{0.35} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-validated weights across 5 folds. DIF weight ranges 0.00--0.08, consistently suppressed by optimization.}
\label{tab:cv-weights}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Weight} & \textbf{CV Std} \\
\midrule
DIF & 0.03 & --- \\
ConStat & 0.62 & --- \\
DVD & 0.35 & --- \\
\midrule
Ensemble vs.\ ConStat alone & \multicolumn{2}{c}{+0.02 improvement} \\
Cross-val AUROC std & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.09} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The ensemble premise required three complementary detectors. Under analysis, DIF's optimized weight drops to $\sim$0.03 (effectively excluded). 2-of-3 majority voting produces AUROC$=$0.52 because DIF is a random voter. The weighted ensemble (0.63) does not exceed ConStat alone (0.68). The ensemble reduces to ``ConStat with extra steps.''

%% 4.7 RL Robustness
\subsection{RL Robustness: Measuring a Misspecified Method}
\label{sec:exp-rl}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{C\% by cohort composition. Adding RL models changes C\% by $<$2pp---interpretable as robustness or insensitivity to meaningful signal.}
\label{tab:rl}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Cohort} & \textbf{C\%} & \textbf{$\Delta$ from SFT} \\
\midrule
SFT-only & 29.4 & --- \\
+ Alignment RL & 30.8 & +1.4 \\
+ Reasoning RL & 31.5 & +2.1 \\
\midrule
DIF flag overlap (SFT vs.\ RL) & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.72} \\
AUROC (DIF vs.\ ConStat) & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.53 (DeLong $p{=}0.61$)} \\
AUROC (DIF vs.\ Min-K\%) & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.51 (DeLong $p{=}0.74$)} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-model detection comparison for 13 reasoning-RL models. DIF flag membership shows near-chance discrimination against ConStat and Min-K\% scores.}
\label{tab:rl-permodel}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Comparison} & \textbf{AUROC} & \textbf{DeLong $p$} \\
\midrule
DIF vs.\ ConStat & 0.53 & 0.61 \\
DIF vs.\ Min-K\% & 0.51 & 0.74 \\
TOST equivalence & \multicolumn{2}{c}{$p{=}0.08$ (marginal)} \\
$N$ reasoning-RL models & \multicolumn{2}{c}{13} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

C\% varies by $<$2pp across SFT-only, alignment-RL, and reasoning-RL cohorts. This ``stability'' reflects the method's insensitivity to the manipulation rather than genuine robustness---DIF produces $\sim$30\% C\% regardless of cohort composition because the signal is architecture-driven. AUROC comparisons against ConStat and Min-K\% are near chance (0.51--0.53). TOST equivalence marginally fails ($p{=}0.08$) due to the $N{=}13$ limitation.

%% 4.8 Semi-Synthetic Framework
\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation Framework}
\label{sec:exp-semisyn}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Detection metrics by injection probability $\times$ matching strategy. $\Delta$TPR is positive and dose-responsive; external matching controls FPR.}
\label{tab:semisyn}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Injection $p$} & \textbf{TPR (Std)} & \textbf{FPR (Std)} & \textbf{TPR (Ext)} & \textbf{FPR (Ext)} \\
\midrule
0.0 & 0.31 & 0.31 & --- & 0.31 \\
0.3 & 0.52 & --- & --- & --- \\
0.5 & 0.81 & --- & 0.83 & --- \\
0.7 & 0.97 & --- & --- & --- \\
1.0 & 1.00 & 0.59 & --- & 0.32 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$TPR at $p{=}0.5$ (Std) & \multicolumn{4}{c}{+0.508} \\
$\Delta$TPR at $p{=}1.0$ (Ext) & \multicolumn{4}{c}{+0.69} \\
FPR inflation (Std) & \multicolumn{4}{c}{+0.28} \\
FPR inflation (Ext) & \multicolumn{4}{c}{+0.01} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The semi-synthetic framework creates genuine differential signal by construction. MH-DIF detects this signal with $\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 at $p{=}0.5$ using standard matching. External matching eliminates FPR inflation (+0.01 vs.\ +0.28). The dose-response pattern is monotonically increasing from $p{=}0.3$ to $p{=}1.0$. This validates the detection \emph{infrastructure} even though natural temporal DIF is artifactual.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{FPR by matching strategy across injection levels. Standard matching inflates FPR as injection level increases; external matching remains stable.}
\label{tab:fpr-matching}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Injection $p$} & \textbf{FPR (Standard)} & \textbf{FPR (External)} \\
\midrule
0.0 & 0.31 & 0.31 \\
1.0 & 0.59 & 0.32 \\
\midrule
Inflation & +0.28 & +0.01 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% 4.9 Cross-Benchmark: GSM8K
\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication on GSM8K}
\label{sec:exp-gsm8k}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{GSM8K DIF summary vs.\ MMLU. Lower C\% on a more architecturally homogeneous population supports the architecture confound explanation. Semi-synthetic framework validates successfully.}
\label{tab:gsm8k-dif}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{GSM8K} & \textbf{MMLU} \\
\midrule
Temporal C\% & 12.3 & 32.1 \\
External C\% & 38.7 & --- \\
Sanity C\% & 0.4 & 0.21 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.1$, Standard) & +0.78 & +0.69 \\
$\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.1$, External) & +0.49 & --- \\
$N$ models (Step 2) & 33 & 5,227 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

GSM8K temporal C\% (12.3\%) is substantially lower than MMLU (32.1\%), consistent with the architecture-artifact hypothesis: GSM8K's model population is more architecturally homogeneous (primarily decoder-only math models). The semi-synthetic framework ($\Delta$TPR$=$+0.78) confirms portability.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{GSM1K correlation analysis. CI crosses zero; finding is suggestive at best.}
\label{tab:gsm1k}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Statistic} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Pearson $r$ & $-$0.31 & 0.08 \\
Spearman $\rho$ & $-$0.28 & 0.11 \\
Partial $r$ (controlling accuracy) & $-$0.22 & 0.21 \\
Permutation $p$ & --- & 0.09 \\
95\% CI & \multicolumn{2}{c}{[$-$0.58, +0.03]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The GSM1K contamination gap correlation ($r{=}-0.31$, $p{=}0.08$) is marginally non-significant with CI crossing zero. This cannot carry evidentiary weight. After controlling for accuracy, partial $r{=}-0.22$ ($p{=}0.21$) is clearly non-significant. The $N{=}33$ sample is underpowered for this analysis.

%% 4.10 Item Generation and Graceful Degradation
\subsection{Item Generation Without Equating}
\label{sec:exp-itemgen}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Difficulty prediction accuracy by method. Student simulation dominates prompt-based targeting but all methods have substantial error. Equating is infeasible without a fitting IRT model.}
\label{tab:itemgen}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
