%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 5. DISCUSSION
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Three Structural Failure Modes}

Our experiments collectively document three reasons why naive psychometric DIF transfer to LLM populations fails:

\textbf{Architecture heterogeneity.} Different architectures (decoder-only, encoder-decoder, MoE) process items through fundamentally different mechanisms. When pooled, these create systematic DIF that is real statistically but reflects population structure, not temporal instability. Architecture stratification reduces C\% by $\sim$70\% (32.1\%$\to$9.7\%), and Item-Focused Trees select architecture over time as the primary split for 90\% of flagged items.

\textbf{Multidimensional construct violation.} MMLU spans 57 subjects, creating a multidimensional construct that cannot be captured by unidimensional IRT. Bifactor models fail to converge (23 Heywood cases), reduced models show poor fit (RMSEA$>$0.07), and even within homogeneous subpopulations the unidimensional assumption is violated (RMSEA$=$0.063). IRT-based methods (MH-DIF, IRT equating, item banking) are built on an inappropriate foundation for these populations.

\textbf{Misspecification propagation.} Bayesian sophistication (Section~\ref{sec:exp-bayesian}), ensemble fusion (Section~\ref{sec:exp-alternatives}), and cumulative evidence accumulation all fail to rescue the misspecified base model. More sophisticated inference on wrong assumptions produces confident wrong answers (Type-S rate$=$0.31) and ensembles dominated by the one component that uses a different mechanism (ConStat weight$=$0.62, DIF weight$=$0.03).

\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}

The field's standard practice of validating contamination detectors against web-overlap or n-gram labels measures global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment$\approx$1.0 against Li et al.\ labels is expected when most MMLU items have been on Common Crawl since 2020. This is not a limitation of DIF specifically but a fundamental validation methodology gap: any item-level detector validated this way has the same blind spot.

\subsection{What Survives: Semi-Synthetic Framework}

The semi-synthetic $\Delta$TPR framework is independent of whether natural DIF is artifactual. By injecting controlled differential contamination into real response matrices, it provides ground truth that proxy labels cannot. Key properties: (a)~dose-response monotonicity, (b)~external matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.01), (c)~cross-benchmark portability (MMLU $\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508, GSM8K$=$+0.78). This infrastructure is reusable for evaluating future item-level detectors that address the architecture confound.

\subsection{Implications}

\textbf{For benchmark designers.} MMLU exhibits massive measurement redundancy ($\rho \approx 0.997$ after 31\% item removal), which provides robustness against any individual source of item degradation but also suggests the benchmark could be substantially shortened.

\textbf{For contamination detection.} Item-level methods must either (a)~operate within architecturally homogeneous subpopulations, or (b)~use covariates (Item-Focused Trees) to disentangle architecture from temporal effects. Group-level DIF on mixed populations should be abandoned for this purpose.

\textbf{For psychometrics in ML.} Standard IRT is misspecified for LLM populations. Future work should develop LLM-specific psychometric models that account for architecture-dependent response patterns and multidimensional construct structures.

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 6. RELATED WORK
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Benchmark contamination detection.} Web-overlap methods detect whether benchmark items appear in training data. Perturbation-based methods (ConStat) test whether models are sensitive to item reformulations. Generation variance methods (DVD) measure response consistency. Our work differs by applying classical psychometric DIF analysis and characterizing why it fails.

\textbf{DIF in educational testing.} The MH procedure and ETS classification are standard in educational measurement. Our work represents the first large-scale application to LLM populations, documenting structural reasons why the transfer fails.

\textbf{Benchmark stability.} Growing Pains examines benchmark reliability using anchor items. Our DIF analysis complements this by characterizing item-level behavioral changes, though our negative results suggest architecture confounds may also affect anchor-based approaches.

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 7. LIMITATIONS
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations}

\textbf{Single benchmark family.} Our primary analysis uses MMLU; GSM8K replication is partial. Generalization to diverse benchmarks (coding, reasoning, safety) remains unvalidated.

\textbf{MH-only DIF.} We use only MH (uniform DIF). SIBTEST analysis (Section~\ref{sec:exp-arch}) shows 72\% non-overlap with MH, suggesting substantial non-uniform DIF that our primary method misses. MIMIC models and propensity-score matching may address some confounding issues.

\textbf{Semi-synthetic simplification.} Injection flips incorrect$\to$correct responses, a crude approximation of real contamination mechanisms. Real contamination may produce subtler signatures.

\textbf{GSM1K correlation.} The $r{=}-0.31$ finding ($p{=}0.08$, $N{=}33$) does not reach significance and cannot support claims about DIF and contamination.

\textbf{Item generation.} Without IRT equating, principled benchmark regeneration via item replacement is not currently feasible. Student simulation ($r{=}0.61$) provides a heuristic but not a psychometric guarantee.

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 8. CONCLUSION
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}

We applied Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to 5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items and found that the naive 32.1\% temporal instability result is predominantly an architecture heterogeneity artifact. Architecture-stratified analysis reduces this to 9.7\%, Item-Focused Trees confirm architecture as the primary driver, bifactor IRT fails to converge, and Bayesian accumulation converges confidently to wrong answers. These negative results are themselves contributions: they document specific structural failure modes that must be addressed before psychometric methods can be productively applied to LLM populations.

Two positive results survive: (1)~the ground truth meta-critique, which identifies a fundamental blind spot in the field's validation methodology, and (2)~the semi-synthetic $\Delta$TPR framework, which provides reusable infrastructure for evaluating item-level contamination detectors independent of natural DIF validity.

We hope this honest accounting of what fails---and why---helps the community develop psychometric tools that account for the structural properties of LLM populations, rather than assuming classical methods transfer unchanged.

