%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% ABSTRACT
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
Differential Item Functioning (DIF), a classical psychometric tool for detecting item-level behavioral anomalies, has been proposed for detecting contamination-driven instability in LLM benchmarks. We apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and initially observe that 32.1\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. However, architecture-stratified analysis reveals this headline result is predominantly an artifact of population heterogeneity: restricting to decoder-only models reduces temporal DIF to 9.7\%, and Item-Focused Trees identify architecture type---not release date---as the primary splitting variable for 3,847 of 4,259 flagged items. Furthermore, IRT model fit remains poor even within architecturally homogeneous subpopulations (RMSEA$=$0.063, CFI$=$0.84), and bifactor models fail to converge (23 Heywood cases across 57 MMLU subjects), demonstrating that the psychometric framework itself is misspecified for LLM populations. Bayesian accumulation of evidence from the misspecified model converges confidently to wrong answers (Type-S error rate$=$0.31). We document three structural failure modes: (1)~architecture heterogeneity inflates DIF by $\sim$70\%, (2)~multidimensional construct structure violates unidimensional IRT assumptions, and (3)~LLM response patterns violate Item Characteristic Curve smoothness. As positive contributions, we validate a semi-synthetic framework for evaluating item-level contamination detectors ($\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 on MMLU, +0.69 on GSM8K with external matching), and argue that the field's standard validation against web-overlap labels is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection (enrichment$\approx$1.0). Code and data are available upon publication.
\end{abstract}

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 1. INTRODUCTION
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: when ``Model~B outperforms Model~A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under equivalent conditions. As the number of publicly available models grows into the thousands, this assumption becomes increasingly difficult to verify. Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis---a psychometric technique designed to detect items that behave differently across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability---offers a principled framework for testing this assumption at the item level.

We report the results of applying Mantel-Haenszel (MH) DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix, comprising 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items. This constitutes the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our initial finding---that 32.1\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF---appears to support contamination-driven instability as a major threat to benchmark validity. However, systematic investigation reveals this result is primarily an artifact of architecture heterogeneity in the model population.

This paper makes three contributions, each grounded in negative results that we believe are informative for the community:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Empirical characterization of structural failure modes.} We document why naive psychometric DIF transfer to LLM populations fails: architecture heterogeneity inflates DIF by approximately 70\%, IRT models are misspecified even within homogeneous subpopulations, and Bayesian sophistication cannot rescue a misspecified likelihood.
    \item \textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} We argue that the field's standard validation of contamination detectors against web-overlap labels is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection methods. DIF enrichment$\approx$1.0 against such labels is expected, not evidence of failure.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We develop and validate a reusable infrastructure for evaluating any item-level contamination detector via controlled differential contamination injection ($\Delta$TPR methodology), demonstrating its portability across MMLU and GSM8K.
\end{enumerate}

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 2. BACKGROUND
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Background}

\subsection{Differential Item Functioning}

An item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly. The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure tests for uniform DIF by stratifying examinees into ability-matched groups (using total score) and computing an odds ratio across strata. ETS classifies items into three categories based on both statistical significance and effect size $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}|$: A~(negligible, $<$1.0), B~(moderate, 1.0--1.5), and C~(large, $\geq$1.5). We focus on Category~C items as indicators of substantial differential functioning.

\subsection{IRT and Model Fit}

Item Response Theory (IRT) models the probability of a correct response as a function of a latent ability parameter $\theta$ via an Item Characteristic Curve (ICC). Standard fit indices include RMSEA (Root Mean Square Error of Approximation; acceptable $<$0.05, mediocre 0.05--0.08, poor $>$0.08), CFI (Comparative Fit Index; acceptable $>$0.95, mediocre 0.90--0.95), and SRMR (acceptable $<$0.08). Bifactor models posit a general factor plus group-specific factors.

\subsection{LLM Benchmark Contamination Detection}

Existing methods include web-overlap analysis, n-gram matching, perturbation-based approaches (ConStat), and generation variance (DVD). These methods typically validate against web-overlap or n-gram labels as ground truth.

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 3. METHOD
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Method}

\subsection{Data}

We use the METABENCH response matrix: 5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items (binary correct/incorrect). Models span release dates from 2023 to mid-2024, architectures including decoder-only, encoder-decoder, and mixture-of-experts (MoE), and training methods including SFT, alignment RL, and reasoning RL.

\subsection{Cohort Definitions}

\textbf{Temporal cohorts.} Reference: models released before a cutoff date (2023); Focal: models released after (2024-H1). \textbf{Architecture subgroups.} Decoder-only ($n{=}3{,}412$), encoder-decoder, and MoE subsets. \textbf{Within-family pairs.} Llama-2$\to$3, Mistral$\to$Mixtral.

\subsection{MH-DIF Procedure}

For each item, we compute the MH odds ratio across total-score strata, convert to the $\Delta_{\text{MH}}$ scale, and classify using ETS thresholds. C\% denotes the percentage of items classified as ETS Category~C.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Injection}

We inject differential contamination by flipping a fraction $p$ of focal group responses on designated items from incorrect to correct. Detection power is measured as $\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$. Two matching strategies are compared: standard (within-subject total score) and external (total score on other subjects or external benchmark).

\subsection{Architecture Stratification and Item-Focused Trees}

To disentangle architecture effects from temporal effects, we (a)~restrict DIF analysis to architecturally homogeneous subpopulations, and (b)~apply Item-Focused Trees (IFT), which use recursive partitioning on model covariates to identify the primary driver of item response differences.

%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% 4. EXPERIMENTS
%%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experiments}

%% 4.1 Naive Full-Population DIF
\subsection{Full-Population DIF on MMLU}
\label{sec:exp-naive}

We first apply MH-DIF to the full, architecture-mixed population as a baseline.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF detection by cohort definition and item filtering on the full METABENCH-MMLU population. Temporal C\% remains $\sim$30--33\% regardless of negative-discrimination filtering, indicating the signal is not item-quality-driven.}
\label{tab:naive-dif}
