Item-Focused Trees provide model-free, data-driven decomposition of what drives item response differences. Architecture type is the primary split for 3,847 items versus only 412 for release date. When restricted to temporal splits only, the C\%-equivalent is 8.7\%---consistent with the 9.7\% from architecture-stratified DIF. The 412 items where time is the primary split cluster in subjects with genuine temporal curriculum effects. Agreement with categorical DIF is low (0.34), confirming that categorical temporal DIF conflates architecture with time.

%% 4.4 IRT Misspecification
\subsection{Bifactor IRT: Fundamental Misspecification}
\label{sec:exp-bifactor}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Model fit across IRT specifications on the full METABENCH-MMLU data. All specifications show poor fit (RMSEA$>$0.05, CFI$<$0.90). The full 57-factor bifactor model fails to converge.}
\label{tab:bifactor}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Specification} & \textbf{Converged} & \textbf{RMSEA} & \textbf{CFI} & \textbf{Heywood} \\
\midrule
Unidimensional (fallback) & Yes & 0.061 & 0.86 & 0 \\
5-factor reduced & Yes & 0.072 & 0.83 & --- \\
57-factor bifactor & No (847 iter) & --- & --- & 23 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The 57-factor bifactor model (general $+$ 57 subject-specific factors) fails to converge after 847 iterations, producing 23 Heywood cases (negative variance estimates) and 8 subjects with inadmissible parameters. Even reduced specifications show poor fit. Without a fitting bifactor model, anchor items cannot be identified, making IRT equating infeasible. This result demonstrates that standard IRT is fundamentally misspecified for LLM populations due to: (a)~within-architecture correlated residuals violating local independence, (b)~57 subjects exceeding feasible factor structures, and (c)~near-deterministic LLM response patterns violating smooth ICC assumptions.

